{"ex-3B04-1":{"number":"1","title":"Describing wave motion","question":"<p>A student produces waves on still water. He disturbs the water surface for 3 s and the waveform as shown is produced. Find the amplitude <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, wavelength λ, period <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the wave.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a8457ca12bb5c277df5a7cd7.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:209.3px\"/></p>","answer":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.05 m</p><p>λ = 2.4 × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.6 m</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 2 s</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.5 Hz</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>1.5 complete cycles are produced in 3 s.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B04  |  Source PDF p. 9","source":{"name":"3B04 Examples.docx","sha256":"d68a07a152520cde8193ca114e81238747827c72f022b2ce2873d920db808d5f","block":2},"id":"ex-3B04-1","chapter":"3B04"},"ex-3B04-2":{"number":"2","title":"Sketching waveform","question":"<p>Susan flicks a rope up and down continuously to produce a wave with constant frequency and amplitude. She</p><p>starts flicking at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 and a complete</p><p>cycle of the wave is produced at</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/6eb7e95ed60594deedaccfa2.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:232.6px\"/></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.4 s. Sketch the waveform of the</p><p>wave at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.5 s.</p>","answer":"<p>From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.4 s to 0.5 s, the wave travels for 0.1 s.</p><p>As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.4 s,</p><p>0.1 s = 0.25<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>∴ The wave moves to the right by 0.25λ.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/92d927070a3d373cf36a4ef6.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:189.9px\"/></p>","tips":"<p><b>Skill</b></p><p>Problem-solving</p><p>Sketching the waveform after a certain time</p><p class=\"numbered-step\">① Express the time interval in terms of period <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p class=\"numbered-step\">② Find the distance (in terms of wavelength λ) that the wave travels in the time interval.</p><p class=\"numbered-step\">③ Displace the original waveform along the travelling direction by the distance obtained in ② . Then draw the remaining waveform.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B04  |  Source PDF p. 9","source":{"name":"3B04 Examples.docx","sha256":"d68a07a152520cde8193ca114e81238747827c72f022b2ce2873d920db808d5f","block":14},"id":"ex-3B04-2","chapter":"3B04"},"ex-3B04-3":{"number":"3","title":"Wave speed","question":"<p>The waveforms of a wave at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 and</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 4 s are as shown.</p><p>(a) Find the wave speed.</p><p>(b) Find the wavelength of the wave.</p><p>(c) Find the frequency of the wave.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/1da8df9be38e1a0529ca59a3.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:213.1px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.2 m s<sup>–1</sup></p><p>(b) λ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.4 m</p><p>(c) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>λ,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.5 Hz</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B04  |  Source PDF p. 11","source":{"name":"3B04 Examples.docx","sha256":"d68a07a152520cde8193ca114e81238747827c72f022b2ce2873d920db808d5f","block":32},"id":"ex-3B04-3","chapter":"3B04"},"ex-3B04-4":{"number":"4","title":"Wave travelling along two strings connected together","question":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are two uniform strings of the same length, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> heavier than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. They are connected to form a long string. One end of the long string is attached to a pole. A student flicks the other end at a constant frequency to produce a wave as shown.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/be8f57874949711ed13369bd.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:506.6px\"/></p><p>Explain how the wave speed, frequency and wavelength change as the wave travels from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","answer":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> have the same tension as they are connected. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> has a greater mass per unit length than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Therefore, the wave speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> decreases when the wave travels from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>The frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the wave does not change because it is the same as the flicking frequency.</p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>λ, λ decreases because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> decreases and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> remains constant.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B04  |  Source PDF p. 13","source":{"name":"3B04 Examples.docx","sha256":"d68a07a152520cde8193ca114e81238747827c72f022b2ce2873d920db808d5f","block":45},"id":"ex-3B04-4","chapter":"3B04"},"ex-3B04-5":{"number":"5","title":"Motion of particles on a wave","question":"<p>A transverse wave travels to the left along a string. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are particles on the string.</p><p>(a) Describe the motion of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at the</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/f8403bc756c3422964bf11f5.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:313.1px\"/></p><p>instant shown.</p><p>(b) Name two particles that are in phase.</p><p>(c) Name two particles that are in antiphase.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is momentarily at rest.</p><p>Consider the waveform a very short time later.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/eac1abc9e2a590c710f766b7.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:385.9px\"/></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are moving downwards. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are moving upwards.</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> (or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>)</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>B and E are not in phase. Although they are moving upwards at this instant, they reach a crest at different instants.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>A and E are 1.5λ apart. D and E are 0.5λ apart.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B04  |  Source PDF p. 19","source":{"name":"3B04 Examples.docx","sha256":"d68a07a152520cde8193ca114e81238747827c72f022b2ce2873d920db808d5f","block":54},"id":"ex-3B04-5","chapter":"3B04"},"ex-3B04-6":{"number":"6","title":"s–d graphs at different instants","question":"<p>The figure shows the waveform of a</p><p>section of a wave at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are particles on the wave. The period of the wave is 4 s.</p><p>(a) Sketch the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph of the wave from particles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at</p><p>(i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0,</p><p>(ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 1 s.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/d184c9b2ca32470fe2c500b5.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:222.8px\"/></p><p>(b) Find the speed of the wave.</p>","answer":"<p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/fd10c5f18590d995a17111ec.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:316.5px\"/></p><p>(ii)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a4fd74a55a38b76486bfc631.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:265.9px\"/></p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.02 m s<sup>–1</sup></p>","tips":"<p><b>Skill</b></p><p><b>Graph</b></p><p>Constructing an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph Find out the following:</p><p>1 Amplitude</p><p>2 Wavelength</p><p>3 Location of equilibrium positions, crests and troughs (to determine the orientation of the curve)</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The leftmost particle W is used as the reference point, so the s–d graph has the same shape as the waveform.</p><p>A = 2 cm</p><p>λ = 4 × 2 = 8 cm</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>T = 4 s ⇒ 1 s = 0.25T</p><p>The wave has travelled 0.25λ to the right.</p><p>∴ Y (d = 4 cm) is at a crest.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B04  |  Source PDF p. 22","source":{"name":"3B04 Examples.docx","sha256":"d68a07a152520cde8193ca114e81238747827c72f022b2ce2873d920db808d5f","block":74},"id":"ex-3B04-6","chapter":"3B04"},"ex-3B04-7":{"number":"7","title":"Reading an s–t graph","question":"<p>The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph (upwards as positive) of a particle on a wave is as shown.</p><p>(a) What is the amplitude of the wave?</p><p>(b) To which direction is the particle</p><p>moving at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.2 s?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/0138678a4a058eda3ffdb278.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:224.4px\"/></p><p>(c) If the wavelength of the wave is 50 cm, what is the wave speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) 10 cm</p><p>(b) Positive direction (upwards)</p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.25 m s<sup>–1</sup></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>s is increasing at t = 0.2 s.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B04  |  Source PDF p. 24","source":{"name":"3B04 Examples.docx","sha256":"d68a07a152520cde8193ca114e81238747827c72f022b2ce2873d920db808d5f","block":104},"id":"ex-3B04-7","chapter":"3B04"},"ex-3B04-8":{"number":"8","title":"Drawing s–t graphs of different particles","question":"<p>A transverse wave travels at 0.5 m s<sup>–1</sup> towards the left. The period of the</p><p>wave is 0.2 s. Its waveform at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 is as shown. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>O</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are</p><p>particles on the wave.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/724253b8846940972a4f28af.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:343.9px\"/></p><p>Take the upward direction to be positive.</p><p>(a) Sketch the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph of particle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.4 s.</p><p>(b) Sketch the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graphs of particles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>O</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> on the same graph obtained in (a).</p><p>(c) Find the wavelength λ of the wave.</p>","answer":"<p>(a)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/f97854f40b23694f70f9eb66.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:410.9px\"/></p><p>(b)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/5caf2b87d238c862c6ca9f64.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:410.6px\"/></p><p>(c) λ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.5 × 0.2 = 0.1 m</p>","tips":"<p><b>Skill</b></p><p><b>Graph</b></p><p>Constructing an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph Find out the following:</p><p>1 Amplitude</p><p>2 Period of vibration</p><p>3 Moving direction of the particle at a certain instant</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>A = 0.3 m, T = 0.2 s</p><p>At t = 0, s = 0</p><p>Wave travels to the left</p><p>⇒ M moving downwards</p><p>⇒ s becomes negative</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>P in phase with M</p><p>⇒ same s–t graph</p><p>O in antiphase with M</p><p>⇒ moving upwards at t = 0</p>","think":"<p>Does the s–t graph of N depend on the travelling direction of the wave?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>No, for the waveform shown. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, N is at a trough, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, and is momentarily at rest. It subsequently moves upwards whichever way the wave travels. For the same amplitude and period its displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>cos</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> in both cases.</p>","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B04  |  Source PDF p. 25","source":{"name":"3B04 Examples.docx","sha256":"d68a07a152520cde8193ca114e81238747827c72f022b2ce2873d920db808d5f","block":119},"id":"ex-3B04-8","chapter":"3B04"},"ex-3B04-9":{"number":"9","title":"Different graphs of wave motion","question":"<p>A vibrator is connected to one end of a long string. It vibrates to generate a transverse travelling wave of wavelength 0.6 m along the string. The figure shows the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph of particle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at a distance 0.9 m from the vibrator.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/dff2ddca27a233b8961f51be.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:392.1px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the amplitude of the wave.</p><p>(b) Sketch the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph for the wave from the vibrator to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.8 s.</p><p>(c) Sketch how the kinetic energy of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> changes from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 1.6 s.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) 0.2 m</p><p>(b)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a9b3424ef375dae1b0f16c95.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:380.6px\"/></p><p>(c)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/5b6de8c96aeedb32add7c285.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:262.1px\"/></p><p>Exercise 4.4 Q5 (p.29)</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>A = 0.2 m, λ = 0.6 m</p><p>Consider P (d = 0.9 m).</p><p>At t = 0.8 s,</p><p>s = 0 and is decreasing</p><p>⇒ trough is approaching</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>s = 0 (at t = 0, 0.8 s, 1.6 s)</p><p>⇒ highest speed</p><p>⇒ KE max</p><p>s = 0.2 m (at t = 0.4 s, 1.2 s)</p><p>⇒ momentarily at rest</p><p>⇒ KE = 0</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B04  |  Source PDF p. 27","source":{"name":"3B04 Examples.docx","sha256":"d68a07a152520cde8193ca114e81238747827c72f022b2ce2873d920db808d5f","block":157},"id":"ex-3B04-9","chapter":"3B04"}}